<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Negative Angles and Trigonometric Ratios Using the CAST Rule</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>An angle \(\theta = [[if test="is(unit=1)"]]{@theta@}[[else]]{@thetadeg@}^\circ[[/if]]\) is in standard position. (A negative angle is measured <strong>clockwise</strong> from the positive \(x\)-axis.)</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> State the quadrant in which the terminal side of \(\theta\) lies. </p>
<div>[[input:ans0]] [[validation:ans0]] [[feedback:prtq]]</div>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Find the reference angle \(\alpha\) of \(\theta\), in [[if test="is(unit=1)"]]radians[[else]]degrees[[/if]]. </p>
<div>\(\alpha = \) [[input:ans_ref]] [[validation:ans_ref]] [[feedback:prtr]]</div>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> Hence, using the CAST rule, find the exact values of: </p>
<div>(i) \(\sin\theta = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prts]]</div>
<div>(ii) \(\cos\theta = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prtc]]</div>
<div>(iii) \(\tan\theta = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prtt]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><strong>(a) The quadrant</strong></p>
<p>\(\theta = [[if test="is(unit=1)"]]{@theta@}[[else]]{@thetadeg@}^\circ[[/if]]\)[[if test="is(unit=1)"]], which is \({@thetadeg@}^\circ\)[[/if]]. Because \(\theta\) is negative, start on the positive \(x\)-axis and turn <em>clockwise</em> through \({@a@}^\circ\). A clockwise turn of \({@a@}^\circ\) ends in the same place as an anticlockwise turn of \(360^\circ - {@a@}^\circ = {@pos@}^\circ\), which lies [[if test="is(qP=1)"]]between \(0^\circ\) and \(90^\circ\)[[/if]][[if test="is(qP=2)"]]between \(90^\circ\) and \(180^\circ\)[[/if]][[if test="is(qP=3)"]]between \(180^\circ\) and \(270^\circ\)[[/if]][[if test="is(qP=4)"]]between \(270^\circ\) and \(360^\circ\)[[/if]]. So the terminal side lies in <strong>Quadrant {@qn@}</strong>.</p>

<p><strong>(b) The reference angle</strong></p>
<p>The reference angle \(\alpha\) is the acute angle between the terminal side and the \(x\)-axis (green in the diagram).
[[if test="is(qP=1)"]]In Quadrant I it is the angle itself: \(\alpha = {@pos@}^\circ\).[[/if]][[if test="is(qP=2)"]]In Quadrant II, \(\alpha = 180^\circ - {@pos@}^\circ = {@refdeg@}^\circ\).[[/if]][[if test="is(qP=3)"]]In Quadrant III, \(\alpha = {@pos@}^\circ - 180^\circ = {@refdeg@}^\circ\).[[/if]][[if test="is(qP=4)"]]In Quadrant IV, \(\alpha = 360^\circ - {@pos@}^\circ = {@refdeg@}^\circ\).[[/if]]
[[if test="is(unit=1)"]]In radians, \(\alpha = {@refdeg@} \times \dfrac{\pi}{180} = {@refrad@}\).[[/if]] So</p>
\[ \alpha = [[if test="is(unit=1)"]]{@refrad@}[[else]]{@refdeg@}^\circ[[/if]]. \]

<p><strong>(c) Using the CAST rule</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step 1: which ratios are positive?</strong> The CAST rule tells us which ratios are positive in each quadrant: <strong>A</strong>ll in Quadrant I, <strong>S</strong>ine in Quadrant II, <strong>T</strong>angent in Quadrant III and <strong>C</strong>osine in Quadrant IV. The other ratios are negative. In Quadrant {@qn@}:</p>
<ul>
<li>\(\sin\theta\) is [[if test="is(qP=1 or qP=2)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]],</li>
<li>\(\cos\theta\) is [[if test="is(qP=1 or qP=4)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]],</li>
<li>\(\tan\theta\) is [[if test="is(qP=1 or qP=3)"]]positive[[else]]negative[[/if]].</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 2: find the ratios of the reference angle.</strong> From the special triangles,</p>
\[ \sin {@refdeg@}^\circ = {@sin_ref@}, \qquad \cos {@refdeg@}^\circ = {@cos_ref@}, \qquad \tan {@refdeg@}^\circ = {@tan_ref@}. \]
<p><strong>Step 3: attach the signs.</strong></p>
\[ \text{(i)}\ \sin\theta = {@sin_theta@}, \qquad \text{(ii)}\ \cos\theta = {@cos_theta@}, \qquad \text{(iii)}\ \tan\theta = {@tan_theta@}. \]
<p><strong>Check.</strong> \(\dfrac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} = \dfrac{ {@sin_theta@} }{ {@cos_theta@} } = {@tan_theta@} = \tan\theta\), as expected. The identities \(\sin(-x) = -\sin x\), \(\cos(-x) = \cos x\) and \(\tan(-x) = -\tan x\) give the same values.</p>
<div style="text-align:center;" role="img" aria-label="Unit circle with the CAST letters in the four quadrants, the angle theta measured clockwise, the point P where its terminal side meets the circle, and the reference angle alpha.">
[[jsxgraph width="360px" height="360px"]]
(function() {
  var a = {#a#}, unit = {#unit#}, q = {#qP#};
  var th = -a*Math.PI/180, px = Math.cos(th), py = Math.sin(th);
  var b = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {boundingbox:[-1.45, 1.45, 1.45, -1.45], axis:false, grid:false,
      keepaspectratio:true, showNavigation:false, showCopyright:false});
  b.create('arrow', [[-1.35, 0], [1.35, 0]], {strokeColor:'#444', fixed:true, highlight:false});
  b.create('arrow', [[0, -1.35], [0, 1.35]], {strokeColor:'#444', fixed:true, highlight:false});
  b.create('text', [1.3, -0.13, 'x'], {fixed:true});
  b.create('text', [-0.15, 1.3, 'y'], {fixed:true});
  /* CAST letters: which ratios are positive in each quadrant */
  var cast = {fixed:true, fontSize:22, color:'#7b1fa2', anchorX:'middle', anchorY:'middle'};
  b.create('text', [ 1.15,  1.15, 'A'], cast);
  b.create('text', [-1.15,  1.15, 'S'], cast);
  b.create('text', [-1.15, -1.15, 'T'], cast);
  b.create('text', [ 1.15, -1.15, 'C'], cast);
  var O = b.create('point', [0, 0], {name:'O', fixed:true, size:2, color:'#222',
      label:{offset:[px > 0 ? -14 : 6, py > 0 ? -12 : 10]}});
  b.create('circle', [O, 1], {strokeColor:'#1565c0', strokeWidth:2, fixed:true, highlight:false});
  var A  = b.create('point', [1, 0],  {visible:false, fixed:true});
  var Am = b.create('point', [-1, 0], {visible:false, fixed:true});
  var P = b.create('point', [px, py], {name:'P', fixed:true, size:4, color:'#c62828',
      label:{offset:[px > 0 ? 10 : -22, py > 0 ? 14 : -16], fontSize:16}});
  var Fp = b.create('point', [px, 0], {visible:false, fixed:true});
  b.create('segment', [O, P], {strokeColor:'#c62828', strokeWidth:2, fixed:true});
  b.create('segment', [O, Fp], {strokeColor:'#1565c0', strokeWidth:4, fixed:true});
  b.create('segment', [Fp, P], {strokeColor:'#2e7d32', strokeWidth:4, fixed:true});
  /* theta is negative: the arc runs clockwise from the positive x-axis to OP */
  b.create('angle', [P, O, A], {radius:0.22, name:'θ', fillColor:'#ffb74d', strokeColor:'#ef6c00',
      fillOpacity:0.3, fixed:true, label:{color:'#ef6c00'}});
  var refStyle = {radius:0.5, name:'α', fillColor:'#81c784', strokeColor:'#2e7d32', fillOpacity:0.35, fixed:true};
  if (q == 1) { b.create('angle', [A, O, P], refStyle); }
  if (q == 2) { b.create('angle', [P, O, Am], refStyle); }
  if (q == 3) { b.create('angle', [Am, O, P], refStyle); }
  if (q == 4) { b.create('angle', [P, O, A], refStyle); }
})();
[[/jsxgraph]]
</div>]]></text>
